      "writtenOn": "2024-01-10T18:22:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "First of all: For the record, I would like to formally protest bending over and paying with a perf penalty for some odd tooling and policy restrictions :) - an unsafe cast is totally fine here in principle.\n\nThat said - yes, turns out, the opaque wrapper is possible. I first thought this would get very hairy as such an opaque Rust-side type that holds the color stop slice would require a new include header for the `skpath_bridge.h` but that\u0027s not the case as I found in the CXX tutorial, and it can just be forward declared.\n\nSo, reworked this (diff is [1]) to remove unsafe blocks and have a size retrieval, and an iterator method on an opaque wrapper type called `BridgeColorStops`. This may look like an odd combination: Why not just a get() method, one might ask. But we were anyway thinking about making the color stops an iterator on the Rust side, compare https://github.com/googlefonts/fontations/blob/main/skrifa/src/color/mod.rs#L118 - so I thought I\u0027d go with an iterator here.\n\nI did not move more of the `populateStopsAndColors` into Rust as I don\u0027t want to handle more color information there, and the conversion from an `SkColor` to an `SkColor4f` is something that should remain on the Skia side.\n\n[1] https://skia-review.googlesource.com/c/skia/+/797756/1..3/",
      "parentUuid": "8f71e385_5fc0f8bc",
      "revId": "eb0f3c1c303025ecd436eaa20de544074ed1a9de",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
